#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519648} :activity/id #uuid "b43c564a-026d-55f8-9ded-681ee43fa117" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560 :topic/id #uuid "539a047f-951c-509f-971e-e2ba69953b90" :topic/math-academy-id 2117} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b304ac-2b36-5437-b9d8-5fcf23fa4d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369471} {:answer/id #uuid "a879fd91-6ea0-5ab3-ad43-e4706f2d1bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369472} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f30d72-4cc9-59bd-b370-c87bbec40944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369473} {:answer/id #uuid "a8834855-579a-54da-8ae2-aa6847585195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} {:answer/id #uuid "253604d5-d42a-5d84-ab1b-eb721ad3403c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} :answer-field/id #uuid "671458dc-075f-5c3e-9fa3-8839124a034e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f6dbe2c-628b-581d-a31e-93679dd1a545" :question/math-academy-id "q-126863" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3562daf036c74c1472931a626d41f28de81e723377597700342823d7f2d1ac2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/52/527eb8572b90c3d2a4449b69015a9b94f4d121b094be6629e1b92c45f2ff0eb0.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =1+7+(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02b0f97c-a1c4-5de3-9e6c-ca0b67ea4390" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b43056-e8dd-536d-b56a-12ac2800c12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} {:answer/id #uuid "ca09836f-3c6e-5b5f-841d-68ede18363c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369458} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3caf47-5329-54b1-8c87-bcf0da3ef96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369459} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b5b1dc-7bc7-5bb9-8b23-063bc496f471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369460} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2e77d0-1779-5c5c-9e64-d3cabd705415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} :answer-field/id #uuid "175c17d1-364f-5ab7-a6b1-2ce4de237b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "017f8f34-6e37-562a-87cf-df5a06a7e434" :question/math-academy-id "q-126860" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5a42039e2501a3909062fd6ed92e0879df03f58fa85a62b5e738e8abc78b903.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-5y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-5,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-5))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}13\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =13\\cdot ({8}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot (64-36) \\\\  & =13\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =364.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48cb7736-a399-57ef-8551-b03d268a4144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b95dc2-7fee-5f7c-b4ec-8675b76cbf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441601} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ef271c-da7e-55bf-8af4-8d0b76cc331e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441603} {:answer/id #uuid "d98c6427-b31b-5669-b745-810c5329aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441605} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bbcaca-d001-596d-9fe9-529dd184cce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441607} {:answer/id #uuid "fedc8124-d82e-59e9-81de-9b44a8c6f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441610} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c819006-1c8a-567c-839e-c05698400658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4082a24-9ee0-5ed5-bf60-7857736d7c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126871" :question/problem "![](images/69/69b42001c8ac5e95e18d89785e73fbba3e78554f9146e59bb50dd618b105f10c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2} \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7855bb63-c81b-53d8-a3eb-d50bbb715b43"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} {:answer/id #uuid "2da41db7-6d0c-5183-9ea5-02fe84e79cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431292} {:answer/id #uuid "3b720f7a-85ac-5b6e-aa55-b3fe1bb64322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431298} {:answer/id #uuid "750e0059-fe60-559a-b87a-afcd74ddf718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431312} {:answer/id #uuid "8173bde3-749c-53ab-95a4-d623b09672f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6feb552-f724-5d46-86df-32005b0ffcc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c72e07b-dd70-5ff7-8f10-0939e87dedc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126855" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b29823e-1d02-5228-87de-cfdd6eddac82" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0662a46f-bec7-54ca-b240-f60d5d5f1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} {:answer/id #uuid "0e19b2e0-2b38-57c0-b50d-3e99a4fb345c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519013} {:answer/id #uuid "12efe453-d9bc-5e34-bdeb-04e1a1d22aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519022} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fe6c9c-6868-551d-8a19-e70fb67ab8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519256} {:answer/id #uuid "d13ea829-20bc-5000-ad9a-b878fad5ad6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} :answer-field/id #uuid "69041780-f401-5aab-a5e0-f58cc39dca02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "881df761-fca2-5afa-aea8-d6a6e63a7521" :question/math-academy-id "q-126866" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1dd3c66e3bf7d25f286720b360d6af1d6361804ab3ff52835d94f78e5084ee.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dcca2676-1c39-51b2-9647-11d87db779a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519647}}] :activity/title "Extending Green's Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518984}]}
